"""Concept lessons in syllabus order. The ¶ marker separates teaching paragraphs."""
LESSONS={}
def put(n,raw,headings=None):
    LESSONS[n]=[]
    for index,block in enumerate(raw.strip().split("\n\n")):
        prose,trace=block.strip().split(" || ")
        LESSONS[n].append(dict(prose=prose.replace(" ¶ ","\n\n"),trace=trace.split(" / ")))
        if headings:LESSONS[n][-1]['heading']=headings[index]

put(2, '''Suppose we are asked to build a social feed. Before estimating servers, we need to know what people will do: publish posts, follow accounts, read updates, or report harmful content. Following one of these activities from the user's intention to its result gives us a user journey. Each journey causes different work inside the system. ¶ We might ask the team, “If publishing works but followers cannot see the post, has the feature succeeded?” The answer determines what we must test. A requirement connects a person's intention to an observable result; a feature name such as “posting” leaves too much undecided. ¶ Write the required outcome for each important journey. “Keep an accepted post and show it to followers within five seconds” is specific enough to discuss and test. Then separate essential behaviour from desirable improvements. Investigate requirements whose failure would cause the greatest harm or whose feasibility is uncertain. Record exclusions too: private messaging should not silently enter an estimate for a public feed. || Journey: publish a post / Must: retain accepted post / Target: visible within 5 s / Exclude: direct messages

Opening a feed appears to be one action. In an assumed session, however, the browser sends one feed request, fetches twenty images, and records one analytics event. The page first needs a list of posts, then the images named in that list. An estimate that counts only the page opening misses most of these requests. Drawing this short sequence helps us find the work before putting numbers against it. ¶ A workload describes the mixture of operations and how often they arrive. For each operation, estimate its rate, the size of the data it carries, and the other components it needs. A small response can still require expensive computation; an image can require little computation but substantial transfer. Keep these workloads separate when finding bottlenecks: resources whose limits restrict useful work. The first estimates are assumptions. Replace them with measurements once the application is running. || 1 feed request / 20 image requests / 1 analytics event / Separate workloads and budgets

Assume users read ten feeds for every post they publish. At the public API, this looks read-heavy. But suppose publishing saves one durable post and then copies a reference into a thousand follower feeds. That one action causes a thousand additional storage writes. ¶ Imagine two authors: one has ten followers and another has a million. The same publish button can cause very different amounts of work. Counting posts alone hides this difference. We need both the frequency of the action and the amount of work that each action produces. ¶ A read/write ratio therefore needs a boundary: are we counting user actions, API requests, or database operations? Creating follower feeds in advance moves work to publication time. Building them when requested moves work to reading time. The choice depends on how often users publish and read, as well as the distribution of follower counts. Additional database copies used to serve reads, called read replicas, can help some reads. If their updates are delayed, however, they may not immediately show a user's new post. || 1 publish action / 1 durable post write / 1000 follower-feed writes / Logical ratio differs from storage ratio

Suppose a server receives 200 requests each second and completes 180. If all the remaining requests stay queued, 200 − 180 = 20 more unfinished requests accumulate every second. After ten seconds, about 20 × 10 = 200 requests remain unfinished, assuming an empty starting queue and no rejection or abandonment. Reporting only the completion rate would hide the growing wait. ¶ Latency is the time one operation takes within a stated boundary. From the caller's perspective, it includes waiting as well as execution. Throughput is the amount of work completed per unit time. A server can keep producing the same number of answers each second while users experience progressively longer delays. ¶ RPS means requests per second, QPS queries per second, and TPS transactions per second. State which work each number counts, since one request may cause several queries. Measure offered work, successful completions, errors, and rejected requests together. Otherwise, an apparently steady throughput can conceal users who are waiting or giving up. || Offered: 200 requests/s / Completed: 180 requests/s / Backlog: +20 requests/s / Measure rejection and timeout too

Consider 100 assumed request times: 99 requests take 10 milliseconds each, and one takes 1,000 milliseconds. The first group contributes 99 × 10 = 990 ms. Adding the slow request gives 1,990 ms, so the mean is 1,990/100 = 19.9 ms. That average tells us little about the user who waited a full second. ¶ To find the median, arrange the observations from fastest to slowest and look at the middle. For these 100 observations, the two middle values are both 10 ms, so the median is 10 ms. A percentile names a position in this ordered set. Under the nearest-rank convention, p95 is the 95th value and p99 is the 99th value. Both are 10 ms here: even p99 can miss the single slowest request. ¶ The slow end of the distribution is called its tail. Inspect the distribution and its extreme observations together; percentile estimates also depend on the convention and sample size. To find a fleet-wide p99, combine compatible observations or histograms, which count observations in latency ranges. Averaging individual servers' p99 values does not produce the fleet p99. Account for timed-out requests rather than discarding them. || 99 requests at 10 ms / 1 request at 1000 ms / Mean = 19.9 ms / Tail experience remains 1000 ms

A payment status page can respond quickly while showing yesterday's information. Its ability to respond and the freshness of its answer are separate properties. ¶ Ask four separate questions about the status page. Can it answer now? Is its answer permitted by the reading rules? Will a committed payment survive the failures we promised to tolerate? Does the service keep working correctly over time? Answering one question does not settle the other three. ¶ Availability concerns providing the required service when needed. Durability concerns whether committed data survives specified failures. Reliability usually concerns correct service over time; state how it is being measured. Consistency defines which observations of state are allowed. For example, must the next read show a completed payment? Choose the requirement for each operation. A popularity count may tolerate delayed updates, while selling the last ticket requires stronger coordination. A single label such as “reliable database” leaves these decisions unanswered. || Request responds successfully / Response may be stale / Acknowledged state may be lost / Test each promise separately

Suppose we want to know whether people can use a service successfully. A service-level indicator, or SLI, is a measured property of the service, such as the fraction of eligible requests that succeed. A service-level objective, or SLO, gives that indicator a target and measurement window: for example, 99.9% successful requests over a month. A service-level agreement, or SLA, is an agreement with stated obligations and possibly consequences. ¶ Assume this service handles one million eligible requests in the month. A 99.9% success target leaves 100% − 99.9% = 0.1% for failures. Since 0.1% is 0.001 as a fraction, the allowance is 1,000,000 × 0.001 = 1,000 failed requests. This is its event-based error budget for that population and period. After 600 eligible failures, 1,000 − 600 = 400 remain within the target. ¶ Define eligible requests and successful outcomes before measuring them. Excluding the failures we meant to track would defeat the purpose. A time-based objective uses time as its denominator and gives a different budget. Budget consumption can guide releases and reliability work, but failures concentrated during checkout may require action even while some budget remains. || 1000000 eligible events / Target: 99.9% good / Allowance: 1000 bad events / Track rate of budget consumption

Suppose stored data grows by an assumed 10% each month. Starting with one unit of storage, the first month ends with 1 × 1.1 = 1.1 units. The next ends with 1.1 × 1.1 = 1.21 units. Each increase applies to the new, larger total. After twelve months, repeated multiplication gives 1 × 1.1¹² ≈ 3.14 units. This is compound growth. ¶ The result is a planning scenario, not evidence that request traffic and CPU usage will also triple. Plan each important resource separately: stored history, frequently accessed data, background jobs, and spare capacity during failures. Include temporary space needed while old and new data layouts coexist during a migration. ¶ Suppose storage is likely to fill first. We need to act early enough to add capacity before that happens, including the time needed to purchase, prepare, and move data onto it. A useful capacity plan therefore names the limiting resource, the lead time for expansion, and the observation that triggers action. Revisit those assumptions as measurements arrive. || Starting volume: 1 unit / Monthly multiplier: 1.1 / After 12 months: 3.14 units / Recheck traffic and resource mix

Assume ten million daily users each make fifty API requests. The daily total is 10,000,000 users × 50 requests/user/day = 500,000,000 requests/day. A day contains 24 × 60 × 60 = 86,400 seconds, so the average rate is 500,000,000/86,400 ≈ 5,787 requests per second. ¶ For this daily workload, separately assume that each user publishes one 2 kB post and views ten 200 kB images per day. These daily assumptions differ from the single-session example earlier. Using decimal units, 2 kB is 2,000 bytes, so raw post growth is 10,000,000 × 2,000 = 20,000,000,000 bytes/day, or 20 GB/day. Image delivery is 10,000,000 × 10 × 200,000 = 20,000,000,000,000 bytes/day, or 20 TB/day. ¶ Stored bytes accumulate while they are retained; delivered bytes measure transfer. Spreading 20 TB evenly across the day gives about 231.5 MB/s, or 1.85 gigabits/s because each byte contains eight bits. This average bandwidth excludes protocol overhead and bursts. Add indexes, extra copies, retained history, and transfer overhead to the relevant resource estimates. ¶ Memory depends on the data kept active, including caches and in-flight requests. CPU demand depends on the work each operation performs, which we must measure. Neither follows from the user count alone. These assumed workloads give us inputs for testing, not company benchmarks or a complete server count. || API: 500 million requests/day / Posts: 20 GB raw/day / Images: 20 TB delivered/day / Add indexes, replicas and headroom

A daily average spreads all requests evenly across 24 hours. Real users may arrive together after a notification or event. Suppose, for planning, that peak API traffic is five times the preceding average. The estimate becomes 5 × 5,787.04 ≈ 28,935 requests per second. Five is an assumed multiplier; a measured traffic trace would give us better evidence. ¶ Peak duration matters too. Suppose a spike adds 1,000 requests each second beyond current capacity for 30 seconds. Without rejection or extra completions, it adds 1,000 requests/s × 30 s = 30,000 queued requests. The service must later complete this backlog, so the wait can continue after the incoming rate falls. ¶ A bounded queue can absorb some temporary excess if the resulting wait is acceptable. Existing spare capacity and admission limits must cover the interval before extra instances are ready. Instances that take several minutes to start may arrive after a short spike ends. Image traffic and posting traffic may peak differently, and retries during an outage can add attempts that ordinary user averages miss. || Average: 5787 requests/s / Assumed peak multiplier: 5 / Peak: 28935 requests/s / Scaling delay still needs protection

Imagine a stable service completing 500 operations per second, with arrivals balanced by completions over a sufficiently long period. Each operation spends an average of 40 milliseconds inside the boundary we measure, including any waiting there. We want to estimate how many operations are present inside that boundary at once. ¶ Little's Law relates three averages. Let L be the average number of operations in the system, λ the long-run arrival rate, and W the average time an operation spends inside it. Then L = λW. Convert 40 ms to 0.04 seconds to match the rate: 500 operations/s × 0.04 s = 20 operations. About twenty are present on average, either waiting or being served. This does not mean there are exactly twenty at every moment. ¶ If mean residence time rises to 0.4 seconds while stable throughput remains 500 operations/s, the average number present becomes 500 × 0.4 = 200. Longer residence leaves more work in progress. Use consistent boundaries and units: a rate measured at the API cannot simply be combined with time spent only inside the database. The relation describes stable averages; it neither predicts p99 nor makes a queue with sustained excess arrivals stable. || Arrival/completion rate: 500/s / Mean residence: 0.04 s / Mean work in flight: 20 / At 0.4 s: 200 in flight

Suppose a job takes 100 seconds on one worker. Twenty seconds must run sequentially, meaning that this part cannot be divided among workers. The other eighty seconds can be shared perfectly. With four workers, the shareable part takes 80/4 = 20 seconds. Total time becomes 20 + 20 = 40 seconds, so the ideal speedup is 100/40 = 2.5 times. ¶ This is an application of Amdahl's Law for a fixed amount of work. Expressed as fractions of the original time, the sequential part is 0.2 and the parallel part is 0.8. The new duration is 0.2 + 0.8/4 = 0.4 of the old duration. Even infinitely many workers cannot remove the sequential twenty seconds, giving an ideal ceiling of 100/20 = five times. ¶ The calculation assumes no extra cost from dividing and coordinating the work. Such costs can reduce the real benefit. Find the stage that limits progress before adding workers. After improving it, measure again: another stage may become the new bottleneck. || Serial fraction: 0.2 / Parallel fraction: 0.8 / 4 workers: 2.5x ideal speedup / Infinite workers: 5x ceiling

A page request may start fifty calls in parallel and need all their results. Its completion depends on the slowest required call: forty-nine quick answers cannot compensate for a missing last answer. Making several calls from one request is called fan-out. ¶ Assume each call independently has a 1% chance of exceeding a chosen time threshold. Each therefore has a 99%, or 0.99, chance of finishing within it. Independence lets us multiply these probabilities: the chance that all fifty are fast is 0.99⁵⁰ ≈ 0.605. The remaining probability, 1 − 0.605 ≈ 0.395, means about 39.5% of page requests have at least one slow required call in this model. Shared dependencies can correlate delays, so independence must be justified. ¶ We can sometimes change which calls are required: recommendations might arrive later while the main page appears. For the calls that remain required, divide the overall deadline into a latency budget. The illustrated sequential example allows 30 ms for network travel, 20 ms for waiting, 100 ms for data access, and 50 ms for computation and the response: 200 ms altogether. Real overlap and variability need measurement. Sending duplicate calls to reduce waiting spends extra capacity and requires repeated execution to be safe. || 50 required parallel calls / Per-call slow probability: 0.01 / All fast probability: 0.99^50 / At least one slow: about 39.5%

Suppose two designs both handle the expected traffic. One is cheaper but allows a tenant's cached result to reach another tenant. It fails a security requirement and should be rejected before comparing prices. ¶ Write mandatory requirements as pass-or-fail checks first. “A tenant must never receive another tenant's private result” is one such check. We can then compare cost and ease of operation among the candidates that pass. Otherwise, a cheap but unsuitable candidate can look attractive simply because we assigned a small numerical weight to an essential rule. ¶ Other differences may be preferences: one design costs more money but takes less operating work. Include staffing, maintenance, migration, and recovery in the comparison. A team also needs the skills to run what it builds. Compare candidates against the same workload and failure requirements. A price advantage obtained by dropping backups or weakening isolation represents a different service, rather than a like-for-like saving. || Check mandatory correctness / Check security and operation limits / Estimate total lifecycle cost / Compare feasible candidates

After rejecting designs that fail mandatory requirements, we may still have several reasonable choices. A decision matrix lists the criteria, gives each candidate a score, and weights the criteria according to their importance. It makes the team's judgments visible. ¶ Suppose cost has weight two and ease of operation has weight one. A candidate scoring three on cost and five on operation receives 2 × 3 + 1 × 5 = 6 + 5 = 11. Here a higher score means a more desirable result, so a higher cost score must mean better affordability. Use that same direction in every column and explain how the scores were chosen. The total summarises these judgments; it does not independently establish them. ¶ Try changing uncertain scores and weights. If a small change reverses the result, seek better evidence or prefer a choice that is easier to reverse. Record the choice, reason, and condition for reconsidering it in an architecture decision record, or ADR. Tests are still needed to establish performance and correctness. A high preference score cannot make an unsupported requirement true. || Reject infeasible candidates / Score explicit preferences / Vary uncertain weights / Record choice and revisit trigger''')

put(3, '''The **browser** is the program we use to view the page. It acts as a **client**: it asks another program for something. The **server** receives that request and sends a response. ¶ Use the invented address `https://tasks.example/tasks`. This complete address is a **URL**. The name `tasks.example` identifies the site. The **path**, `/tasks`, identifies what we want from it. The `https` part asks for protected web communication; we will explain that protection in Chapter 3.2. ¶ The browser finds a network address for the site and sets up communication. It then asks for `/tasks`. In a simplified HTTP request, this is `GET /tasks`. **HTTP**, the Hypertext Transfer Protocol, gives web requests and responses their rules. `GET` asks to read a resource, such as this page. A response marked `200 OK` reports success and carries the page data. The browser uses that data to display our task list. || Browser asks for /tasks / Site name gives a destination / HTTP request reaches application / Response displays the task list

How does the request reach the right program? It needs both a machine's network address and a port number. ¶ An **Internet Protocol (IP) address** identifies a network destination. Suppose our server uses the example address `203.0.113.10`. Data travels in **packets**, small units carrying data and addressing information. A **router** reads a packet's destination address and forwards it along a suitable next link. Repeating this step moves the packet toward the server. Choosing these next links is **routing**. ¶ A **port number** helps the operating system find a listening service on that machine. Our web service listens on port `443`, commonly used for HTTPS. The server’s **operating system**, the software managing its programs and network access, uses the port information to find that listener. The program reads and writes data through a **socket**, a communication object provided by the operating system. ¶ The browser also uses a local address and port. The server needs that information to send replies back to this client. Listening only on `127.0.0.1`, the local loopback address, would instead make that listener available only on its own machine. A remote client also needs working routes in both directions. || Packet names 203.0.113.10 / Router chooses the next link / Port 443 finds the listener / Application reads its socket

**Transport protocols** give programs rules for carrying data between them. **TCP** (Transmission Control Protocol), **UDP** (User Datagram Protocol), and **QUIC** provide different transport arrangements. ¶ TCP provides an ordered **byte stream**. Its connections are distinguished by both ends’ addresses and ports, allowing many clients to use the same listening service. Suppose the sender writes `HELLO` and then `WORLD`. The receiver may read `HELLOWO`, followed by `RLD`. The bytes remain ordered, but the two writes do not become two separate messages. The application needs a **framing** rule, such as sending each message's length before its contents. ¶ TCP numbers positions in the byte stream. Suppose bytes 1–100 arrive, bytes 101–200 are missing, and bytes 201–300 arrive. The receiver cannot yet deliver the later bytes as the next part of the stream. Acknowledgments report receiving progress, and TCP retransmits missing data. The application can receive the continuous ordered stream once the gap is filled. A connection can still fail before all data arrives. ¶ UDP sends separate **datagrams**, whose boundaries are preserved. It supplies neither TCP's ordered stream nor its retransmission service. The application must arrange any recovery it needs. ¶ QUIC runs over UDP and supplies its own encryption, loss recovery, and ordered streams. A missing part of one stream need not stop another stream from delivering its data. The streams still share network capacity. QUIC also uses connection identifiers, which can help a connection remain recognisable when an address changes. None of these transports proves that the receiving application has saved or acted on the data. || TCP: numbered byte stream / UDP: separate datagrams / QUIC: separate ordered streams / Application recognises its messages

We know the name `tasks.example`. How do we obtain its IP address? The **Domain Name System**, or **DNS**, provides records associated with names. ¶ The browser, or the operating system on its behalf, asks a **resolver**, a service that finds DNS answers. If the resolver has no usable saved answer, it follows DNS information to a server responsible for the name. That **authoritative server** supplies the published record. The resolver can then return the address to the client. ¶ A **cache** saves a result for reuse. Suppose the returned record permits caching for 60 seconds. This permitted lifetime is its **time to live**, or **TTL**. A lookup ten seconds later can reuse the saved answer. After its permitted lifetime ends, the resolver normally needs a new answer before reusing that record. ¶ Now change the published address. Existing caches can still hold the earlier answer until it expires. Clients with an open connection may not perform another lookup at all. DNS can also cache negative answers, such as “this name does not exist.” This is why changing a DNS record does not move every client at once. || Resolver asks for a record / Answer saved with 60 s TTL / Published address changes / Old cached answer lasts until expiry

The browser has found a server. It must check that the server really represents `tasks.example` and protect messages as they travel. **HTTPS** uses **Transport Layer Security**, or **TLS**, for this purpose. ¶ A **certificate** is a signed statement linking a name to a public key. The **public key** may be shared; its matching **private key** is kept secret. A **digital signature** made with a private key can be checked with the matching public key. Changes to the signed data make that check fail. ¶ The certificate carries its issuer’s signature. The browser checks a chain of these signatures leading to a certificate authority it trusts. It also checks the requested name and the certificate’s validity. During this initial exchange, the server proves it holds the required private key and the peers establish traffic keys. ¶ The traffic keys protect later HTTP messages. **Encryption** makes their contents unreadable without the relevant keys. **Integrity protection** allows alteration to be detected. The identity check is **authentication**: establishing who the peer is. ¶ This protection ends where TLS ends. If one server decrypts a request and forwards it, the next connection needs its own protection. The application must also check whether this user may read task 42. That permission check is **authorisation**. A protected connection alone cannot answer it. || Check certificate and site name / Server proves its identity / Peers establish traffic keys / Protect later HTTP messages

Our page needs a document and two images. These are three HTTP exchanges: each has a request and a response. A **header** is a named field carrying information about an HTTP message, such as its content type. ¶ HTTP/1.1 can reuse connections, but an earlier slow response can hold up later responses on the same connection. Browsers commonly use several connections. HTTP/2 divides messages into smaller frames and labels them by **stream**, so data for several exchanges can share one connection. This sharing is called **multiplexing**. ¶ HTTP/2 normally carries those frames over TCP. If a missing TCP byte blocks delivery, frames for several streams can wait behind the same gap. HTTP/3 uses QUIC streams, so loss in one stream need not block delivery in another. All three versions still use HTTP meanings such as `GET` and `200 OK`. They change how messages travel, rather than what those operations mean. || HTTP/1.1: ordered responses / HTTP/2: streams share TCP / TCP gap can delay those streams / HTTP/3: separate QUIC streams

A **connection** lets both ends keep information about an ongoing transport conversation. A new TCP connection normally begins with three messages, called a **handshake**. ¶ First, the client asks to connect and announces its starting byte-sequence number: SYN. The server acknowledges that message and announces its own starting number: SYN-ACK. The client acknowledges the server: ACK. Both ends now have the starting information for sending and recognising stream data. For HTTPS over TCP, TLS setup adds the protection described earlier. ¶ Once the task page has arrived, the browser can use the same usable connection again. A request needing fresh setup is often called **cold**; one reusing setup is called **warm**. An idle connection can be closed, so reuse lasts only while the path remains usable. Replacing a failed connection does not tell us whether its last request already ran. || Client: SYN and starting number / Server: SYN-ACK and its number / Client: ACK / Connection ready for stream data

A **round trip** includes travel to another participant and back. If one exchange must finish before the next starts, their waiting times add. ¶ Assume this simplified cold request needs 20 ms for DNS, 40 ms for TCP setup, 40 ms for TLS setup, and 55 ms for the request, server work, and reply. The elapsed totals are 20 ms, 60 ms, 100 ms, and finally `20 + 40 + 40 + 55 = 155 ms`. ¶ If the next request reuses the lookup and secure connection, the assumed setup work disappears. The same request-and-work stage remains: 55 ms. Its travel is already included, so we do not add another round trip to it. ¶ These are teaching inputs, not measured timings or fixed handshake costs. The calculation shows why a short server calculation can still be part of a much longer wait. || DNS: 20 ms / TCP setup: 40 ms / TLS setup: 40 ms / Request and reply: 55 ms

A **proxy** receives communication and forwards it on another party's behalf. The names forward and reverse tell us which side it serves. ¶ A **forward proxy** serves clients. A browser sends its request to the proxy, which contacts the destination on the browser's behalf. An organisation might use this arrangement for staff internet access. ¶ A **reverse proxy** fronts a service. Visitors contact it as the site's public server; it forwards requests to an application server behind it. Such a destination is often called a **backend**. The reverse proxy can handle TLS and select a destination by the requested path. ¶ Forwarding information also needs a trust rule. If the application accepts a “user 7” header, a public caller must not be able to invent that identity. A trusted proxy must verify the identity and remove or replace untrusted versions of that header. The application must also control who can reach it directly. || Forward proxy acts for clients / Reverse proxy fronts servers / Proxy makes the next request / Forwarded identity must be checked

Suppose three copies of our task application can answer requests. A **load balancer** chooses which copy receives traffic. It might send successive requests to A, B, and C so that one copy does not receive all the work. ¶ An **API** is an interface through which programs use application operations. An **API gateway** can perform common checks before requests reach those operations. For example, it may check a sign-in credential or enforce a **quota**, an allowance such as a maximum number of requests. ¶ Follow a request for task 42. The gateway checks who is calling. The balancer selects application B. B then checks whether that user owns task 42 before returning it. These are separate decisions, although one product may perform both gateway and balancing work. All three application copies may still depend on the same database. || Gateway checks the caller / Balancer selects instance B / B checks ownership of task 42 / B returns the permitted task

A balancer can choose a destination using different parts of a request. The familiar labels **Layer 4 (L4)** and **Layer 7 (L7)** refer to the transport and application layers of a network model. ¶ An L4 rule can use IP addresses and transport ports. It can select a server for a TCP connection without reading the HTTP path. Traffic on that connection generally continues to the selected server. ¶ An L7 rule reads application messages. It can send `/tasks` to the task service and `/images` to an image service. To read an encrypted HTTP request, the routing component commonly ends the TLS connection and decrypts the message. The useful distinction is the information used for the choice: transport information or application content. || L4 reads addresses and ports / L7 reads an HTTP path / /tasks selects the task service / Selected connection carries traffic

Our task page needs to show new changes. With **polling**, the browser repeatedly asks whether anything has changed. If 1,000 clients ask every five seconds, the average rate is `1,000 / 5 = 200 requests per second`, even when there are no updates. ¶ With **Server-Sent Events (SSE)**, one HTTP response stays open and the server sends a sequence of events through it. With **WebSockets**, either end can send messages over the established channel. Both can avoid repeated empty polls, while keeping connections and buffers in use. A buffer is memory holding data while it waits to be handled or sent. ¶ Suppose a client receives event 20, disconnects, and misses events 21–23. Reopening a connection does not recover them by itself. The application must send retained events after 20, or send a fresh task list. Small **heartbeat** messages can help discover a broken idle path; they do not replace that missed-update recovery. || 1000 clients poll every 5 s / Average: 200 requests/s / SSE sends server events / WebSocket sends in both directions

A laptop may use private address `10.0.0.7` inside its local network. To reach an internet service, its traffic may pass through **network address translation**, or **NAT**, which changes address information. ¶ For example, a NAT device can replace source `10.0.0.7:51000` with external source `203.0.113.5:62000`. It remembers the mapping. When a reply arrives for the external address and port, it uses that mapping to forward the reply to the laptop. These are illustrative addresses. If the mapping expires, an old connection may no longer have a working return path. ¶ A **firewall** applies rules allowing or blocking traffic. NAT changes addressing; a firewall decides which traffic is permitted. One device can do both. ¶ **Ingress** means traffic entering a boundary; **egress** means traffic leaving it. Our request is egress from the laptop's network and ingress to the server's network. The reply travels the other way. Checking both directions helps explain why a listening server may still receive no usable connection. || Laptop: 10.0.0.7:51000 / NAT: 203.0.113.5:62000 / Reply reaches the NAT mapping / Mapping returns it to the laptop

Our task application may need a file-conversion service. Its instances can be replaced, so their addresses can change. **Service discovery** finds the current destinations for a service name. ¶ An instance can register its address and port in a **registry**, a list of service instances. The caller obtains the list, selects instance A, and connects. DNS or a deployment platform can also provide this information. ¶ Now let A stop after the caller receives the list. The saved list still names A, so the call can fail. The caller needs to refresh the list and handle an unusable destination. Discovery finds a possible place to call; the operation's own rules decide whether a failed call can safely be retried. || Instance A registers its address / Caller obtains the instance list / A stops before the call arrives / Refresh the list after failure

The task page includes a public help image. Its original server is called the **origin**. A **content delivery network (CDN)** keeps permitted copies at serving locations near users. These locations are called **edges**. ¶ The edge looks for the requested image in its cache. Finding a usable copy is a **hit**. Otherwise it is a **miss**: the edge fetches the image from the origin and can save a copy for later requests. A **cache key** identifies which saved copy to look up. Different image sizes need distinguishable keys. ¶ Reuse reduces some travel and origin work. It also needs a freshness rule saying when the copy may be used. A public help image can be shared widely; a private task page cannot be shared just because its URL looks similar. Permission and content differences must be preserved when deciding which requests may share a copy. || Browser requests the help image / Edge finds no usable copy / Edge fetches from the origin / Later hit reuses the saved copy

Our client asks to add “Read” and waits one second. No reply arrives. Reaching the waiting limit is a **timeout**. It tells us the reply was not received in time. ¶ Consider two sequences. In the first, the request never reaches the server. In the second, the server saves task 7 but its reply is lost. The client sees the same timeout in both. Repeating the request as a new operation could add “Read” twice. ¶ Give the first request an operation identifier, `op-9`. The server saves the task and records “op-9 created task 7” in the same database transaction. If the client resends `op-9` with the same request, the server returns that recorded result. The operation identifier recognises the request; the task identifier names the created task. A status lookup can also reveal the result. ¶ Set an overall **deadline**, the latest time the caller will wait, so retries do not restart the full allowance. **Packet loss** means a packet did not reach its destination; a transport may recover it. A **network partition** prevents some participants from communicating even though they may still be running. A timeout alone cannot distinguish these causes from a stopped or slow server. || Request carries op-9 / Server saves task 7 and result / Reply is lost; client times out / Retry op-9 returns task 7

Opening a database connection has a setup cost. A **connection pool** keeps connections ready for reuse. A request borrows one, performs its database work, and returns it so another request can use it. If every connection is borrowed, the next request waits. ¶ Assume twenty connections, each held for an average of 50 ms, or 0.05 seconds. One continuously busy connection can complete about `1 / 0.05 = 20` borrowing periods per second. Twenty give an ideal rate of `20 × 20 = 400 operations per second`, assuming one operation per period and no other limit. ¶ This calculation uses the assumed hold time. Keeping a connection longer lowers the rate. Failing to return one removes a slot from the pool, even when the database is healthy. Returning borrowed connections on success and error therefore matters as much as creating them. ¶ **Keepalive** probes are small checks sent on an idle connection to help discover that it has broken. Reusing an HTTP connection, also sometimes called keep-alive, is a different use of the term. Neither tells us whether a particular application operation completed. || Borrow one of 20 connections / Hold it for 0.05 s on average / Return it for the next request / Ideal rate: 400 operations/s''',
['Follow a request from the browser',
 'Find the machine and the program',
 'Choose how data travels',
 'Find an address from a name',
 'Protect the conversation with TLS',
 'Carry several HTTP exchanges',
 'Use a connection again',
 'Add up the waiting time',
 'Forward a request through a proxy',
 'Share traffic and check common rules',
 'Route by port or by path',
 'Send updates to the browser',
 'Follow traffic out and back',
 'Find a service whose address can change',
 'Reuse a nearby copy',
 'What does a timeout tell us?',
 'Borrow and return connections'])

put(4, '''Our task application now accepts file uploads. The instructions saved on disk form a **program**. When we start it, the operating system creates a **process**, a running instance with memory and resources such as open files. The operating system is the software that manages these resources and access to the processor. ¶ Start the same program twice. We now have two processes. Each has its own running state and its own view of memory, called an **address space**. Ordinary variables in one process are not automatically variables in the other. ¶ Inside a process, a **thread** is a path of execution: a sequence of instructions that can be run. One thread might receive an upload while another works on an earlier file. Threads in the same process can share data. This helps them cooperate, but also lets them interfere with each other's changes. ¶ A source-code module is a way to organise instructions. It need not be a separate process. Several modules can run in one process and stop together if that process crashes. || Instructions saved as a program / Start it: create process A / Start again: create process B / Threads within a process share data

An upload arrives in a **buffer**, an area of memory holding its bytes. A **reference** lets the program refer to that buffer without making another copy of all its contents. ¶ The **call stack** records unfinished function calls and where execution should return. When `handle_upload` calls `read_file`, a new call record is added. When `read_file` returns, its call record is removed. A record can hold local values and references to objects. ¶ Dynamically created objects are commonly kept in an area called the **heap**. Suppose `handle_upload` gives its buffer to background work and then returns. Its call has ended, but the background work still holds a reference. The buffer must remain available until that work stops using it. ¶ Languages manage this storage differently. A **runtime** is the supporting code that carries out language operations. In a runtime with **garbage collection**, memory can be reclaimed when the program can no longer reach an object through the references it keeps. A background task’s reference therefore keeps our buffer available, even after the handler returns. That does not promise prompt closure of a file or database connection. Those resources need a clear close or return step when their use ends. || Handler creates an upload buffer / Background task keeps a reference / Handler returns; its call ends / Buffer remains while task needs it

The **CPU**, or processor, carries out program instructions. A **core** is an execution unit within it. Assume our available core runs one thread at a time. If two threads are ready, they take turns. The operating system's **scheduler** chooses which ready thread runs next. ¶ Suppose A begins reading an upload from storage and must wait. The scheduler can run B during that wait. When the read is ready and A is selected again, A continues from where it paused. ¶ Changing the running thread requires saving enough information to resume it and restoring the next thread's state. This is a **context switch**. Switching itself takes work. If both A and B need continuous calculation, taking turns does not give them a second core. More waiting threads may help use idle time; more ready threads competing for a busy core also add waiting and switching. || A starts a storage read / A waits; scheduler runs B / A becomes ready again / Switch back and resume A

An upload can spend time calculating or waiting for data. **Input/output (I/O)** moves data between the program and something such as a network connection or storage. A workload is **I/O-bound** when these operations are its main limit. It is **CPU-bound** when calculation is the main limit. ¶ Assume 1,000 independent uploads each wait 100 ms for storage and need negligible calculation. Handle them one at a time: `1,000 × 100 ms = 100 seconds`. Allow fifty waits at once: there are twenty ideal groups, taking `20 × 100 ms = 2 seconds`. ¶ The second calculation assumes all fifty waits overlap without making storage slower. Actual bandwidth, overhead, and storage capacity can increase the time. ¶ Now let each file need compression instead. A core must execute the compression instructions. Overlapping more waiting operations does not supply more computing power. First ask where the time goes: calculation, I/O, or both. That tells us which resource needs attention. || 1000 uploads wait 100 ms each / One at a time: 100 seconds / 50 waits at once: 20 groups / Ideal waiting time: 2 seconds

Suppose the caller asks our service to convert a file. With a **synchronous** interface, the call returns the result or an error before the caller continues past it. ¶ With an **asynchronous** interface, starting the operation and receiving its result are separate. The caller can receive a way to obtain the later result, do other work, and wait for completion when it needs that result. ¶ This describes how completion is presented to the caller. A separate question is whether a thread is held waiting underneath. An asynchronous operation can still use a library call that blocks its thread. To understand the behaviour, we must follow what the actual call does while it waits. || Synchronous call waits for result / Asynchronous start returns first / Caller can do other work / Caller obtains the later result

A thread calls `read` to obtain the next upload bytes. If no bytes are available, a **blocking** read keeps that thread waiting inside the call until data or another ending condition is available. The scheduler can run another thread meanwhile. ¶ A **nonblocking** read can return “not ready” instead. The program arranges for a notification when reading may make progress. The thread can then do other work rather than remain in that read. When notified, the program tries the read again. ¶ The next read may supply only part of the file. Reading a few bytes and finishing the entire upload are different events. Some platforms instead report completion of an operation submitted earlier. In either arrangement, repeatedly checking without waiting for a useful notification can waste CPU time. Nonblocking I/O helps because unfinished waits leave the thread available for other work. || Read reports not ready / Arrange notification / Thread does other work / Notification leads to another read

Upload A starts and waits for storage. While A waits, one core runs B. Later it resumes A. Both uploads are in progress during overlapping periods. This is **concurrency**. ¶ Now let two cores execute work on A and B at the same instant. This is **parallelism**. Concurrency is about overlapping progress; parallelism is about simultaneous execution. One core can provide the first without providing the second. ¶ Taking turns can still cause interference. A might read a shared completed-upload count and pause before writing it. B can change the count before A resumes. We will follow that sequence in Chapter 4.3. Shared data needs coordination even when the threads do not execute at the same instant. || A runs, then waits / B runs on the same core / Both are in progress: concurrent / Two cores can run work in parallel

A thread carries out instructions. A **task** represents a piece of work that can pause and resume. A **future** represents a result that may not yet be available; it later holds the result or an error. Languages also use the name **promise** for objects representing later results; their exact interfaces differ. ¶ An **event loop** runs ready pieces of work in turn, often on one thread. A **callback** is a function arranged to run when an event occurs. The loop runs a callback or part of a task, then moves to other ready work. ¶ Follow two uploads. Task A starts a read and reaches `await`, which waits for its result. If the read is unfinished, A pauses. The loop runs B. When A's read completes, its future receives the result and A becomes ready to continue. The code that resumes A is its **continuation**. ¶ Ready does not mean running. If B keeps calculating for two seconds without yielding the loop thread, A waits for its turn even though its bytes have arrived. An `await` on an already available result may not pause at all. What matters is when the running work actually gives the loop a chance to run something else. || Task A awaits its read / Loop runs task B / Read completes; A becomes ready / A resumes when loop gives it a turn

Let a **job** mean “convert this uploaded file.” A **worker** is a thread or process that takes a job, does the work, and takes another. A **worker pool** is a fixed set of these workers. A thread pool uses threads for that role. ¶ Suppose eight workers are busy. New jobs enter a **queue**, a collection waiting to be taken. Eight workers limit active jobs to eight; they do not limit the number waiting. Give the queue its own limit too. ¶ Keep the file bytes in storage and place small file references in the queue. A waiting job then need not keep the whole upload in memory. In our example, a full queue causes the service to reply “busy” instead of accepting another job. ¶ This queue is held in process memory. A restart removes its entries. If “accepted” must mean that processing will survive a restart, save a job record as well as the file, so a replacement worker can find the unfinished work. || Eight workers are busy / New file references enter a queue / Queue reaches its waiting limit / Next job receives a busy response

Two workers finish uploads and need to increase the same completed-file count. With **shared memory**, both can read and change that count directly. They need a common rule for taking turns during an update. ¶ With **message passing**, one owner keeps the count. Worker A sends “one file finished.” The owner takes that message, adds one, and can reply with the new count. It then handles B's message. The workers request changes instead of changing the owner's count themselves. ¶ This arrangement helps only if the owner really controls the state. Passing a reference to a mutable object does not make it private when other callers can still change it. Across a failure, a message may also be repeated or its reply lost. Message passing changes where coordination happens; the chosen delivery and recovery rules still matter. || Workers send completion messages / One owner takes A’s message / Owner increases its private count / Owner handles B’s message next

Start the completed-file count at zero. A reads zero. Before A writes, B also reads zero. A adds one and writes one. B adds one to its earlier value and also writes one. Two completed files have produced a count of one. ¶ This is a **lost update**. The result depends on how the operations take turns, so it is a **race condition**. The error begins with the shared old read, before either final write. ¶ A **critical section** is the part that must be protected from interfering access. Here it includes reading, adding, and writing. With the same suitable lock around all three steps, A changes zero to one; B then reads one and changes it to two. Locking only the assignments leaves both earlier reads unprotected. Every caller changing this protected count must follow the same rule. This is an illustrative interleaving; language memory rules also govern which shared accesses are allowed. || A reads 0 / B reads 0 / A writes 1 / B writes 1: one increment is lost

A **mutex** is a lock held by one caller at a time. A acquires the mutex, updates the count, and releases it. B waits to acquire that same mutex before entering the protected section. Separate private locks would not make A wait for B. ¶ A **semaphore** manages a number of permits. With four permits, the first four uploads can enter; a fifth waits. When one upload returns a permit, another may proceed. Four permits limit resource use but do not give exclusive access to a shared counter. ¶ An **atomic operation** is indivisible for its specified action. An atomic increment combines reading, adding, and writing as one operation for that counter. ¶ Separate atomic actions do not automatically form one larger action. If transferring ten changes balances from `100,100` through `90,100` to `90,110`, another observer can encounter the intermediate pair. One repair uses the same lock for both changes and for readers of the pair, so a reader cannot enter halfway through the transfer. ¶ A **condition variable** lets a worker sleep until shared state may have changed. The worker locks the queue, finds it empty, and waits. Waiting releases the mutex. A producer can then acquire it, add a job, notify a waiter, and release it. ¶ The worker reacquires the mutex and checks the queue again before taking a job. It checks in a loop because another worker may have taken the job first, and some interfaces permit wake-ups without a relevant state change. || Worker locks the empty queue / Wait releases the mutex / Producer adds a job and notifies / Worker reacquires and rechecks

A holds the file lock and waits for the counter lock. B holds the counter lock and waits for the file lock. Each waits for the other to release something, so neither can finish. This is **deadlock**. ¶ Require every caller to acquire the file lock before the counter lock. B can no longer hold the counter lock while waiting for the file lock. This removes the illustrated circular wait. The order must apply to every path that takes both locks. ¶ In **starvation**, one caller keeps missing out while others make progress. For example, A repeatedly obtains a lock before waiting B gets a turn. B can remain stuck even though the system keeps completing A's work. ¶ In **livelock**, callers keep acting but finish nothing. Suppose A and B each take a different lock. Both fail to obtain the second, release the first, and retry in exactly the same pattern. They are active, unlike deadlock, but remain unable to complete. A retry needs to change the failed coordination, rather than repeat it forever. || A holds the file lock / B holds the counter lock / A waits for the counter lock / B waits for the file lock: cycle

A creates an upload record, sets its title, and shares a reference with B. We want B to see the completed title. Merely knowing that A ran earlier does not establish that guarantee. ¶ A language's **memory model** gives rules for which writes another thread is allowed or guaranteed to observe. A **compiler** translates program code into instructions. It and the processor can arrange execution and memory access in ways that an unsynchronised reader cannot safely infer from the source’s line order. A log's timestamps do not add the missing synchronisation. ¶ Consider a mutex with the language's required visibility guarantees. A acquires it, sets the title and shared reference, then releases it. B later acquires the same mutex and reads the record. The release-and-acquire relationship makes A's earlier protected writes visible under those rules. The Go memory model specifies such an ordering for mutex unlock and later lock operations. ¶ **Happens-before** names the ordering relationship used to reason about effects like these. It connects program order with the language's synchronisation rules. The writer's initialisation then happens before the reader's use. This **safe publication** matters even when nobody will change the record afterward. Other languages require their own documented memory-model guarantees. || Writer locks and sets the title / Writer shares reference and unlocks / Reader later takes the same lock / Reader sees the initialised record

A task record that never changes after construction is **immutable**. Two workers can read that record without competing to change its fields, once it has been safely shared with them. ¶ Now put immutable records into a mutable dictionary. Adding or removing an entry still changes the dictionary. The records' immutability does not protect those dictionary operations. ¶ An object is **thread-safe** for a stated use when permitted concurrent calls preserve its required behaviour. For our completed-file counter, two allowed increments must leave the correct count. A suitable lock or atomic increment can provide that behaviour. For a collection, its own access rules must protect lookup, insertion, removal, and any combined operation the application needs. ¶ Ask which part changes and who controls it. Keeping state private to one owner, returning copies, or sharing immutable values can reduce competing changes. Returning a writable internal collection lets callers bypass that protection. || Construct an immutable record / Share it through safe publication / Readers leave its fields unchanged / Changing the collection needs rules

Assume 150 jobs arrive each second and workers finish 100. With no rejection or abandonment, unfinished work grows by `150 − 100 = 50 jobs per second`. After ten seconds, 500 additional jobs remain. Once workers are busy, this excess waits in the queue. ¶ Those jobs retain memory or other resources. Continued growth can cause **resource exhaustion**: the service runs out of something it needs. A larger queue delays the problem without changing the extra fifty arrivals each second. ¶ Now limit the queue to 100 waiting jobs. When it fills, the service can reply “busy,” and the producer can slow down before trying again. Or a producer can wait for queue space before sending more work. This effect on the producer is **backpressure**: downstream capacity limits upstream work. ¶ A queue limit protects space; the response and the producer's reaction make the feedback useful. Also observe how long jobs wait. Even a queue that fits in memory can contain work that is already too old to be useful. || 150 jobs arrive each second / Workers finish 100 each second / Unbounded queue grows by 50/s / Full bounded queue tells producer to wait

One request starts two child tasks. One reads the uploaded file’s name and size; the other makes a preview. The request-handling task is their **parent**. With **structured concurrency**, these related tasks belong to a **scope**, a block of work whose parent must account for its children before leaving. The scope keeps track of their completion and failure. ¶ Suppose the request's time limit ends. The parent requests **cancellation**: it asks the children to stop. A child reaches a stopping point, closes its file, releases any borrowed resources, and ends. The parent accounts for its children's termination before leaving their scope. A blocking call that cannot respond promptly can delay this sequence; cancellation is not an instant kill. ¶ Stopping the connection does not reverse work already saved. If the service accepted a job that must continue after the request ends, give it a saved job record and an owner beyond this request. Request-scoped children can then stop and release their resources while a later worker resumes the accepted job. || Parent starts two child tasks / Time limit requests cancellation / Children stop and release resources / Parent accounts for them and leaves''',
['Start a program and follow its threads',
 'Keep data alive while it is needed',
 'Let threads take turns',
 'Find out whether work calculates or waits',
 'Start now and receive a result later',
 'Wait without holding the thread',
 'Distinguish overlap from simultaneous work',
 'Follow a task through an event loop',
 'Give workers a bounded waiting queue',
 'Share a value or send a message',
 'Follow a lost counter update',
 'Choose the right coordination tool',
 'When work stops making progress',
 'Make a completed value visible to a reader',
 'Keep shared data safe to use',
 'Slow arrivals when workers cannot keep up',
 'End related tasks and release their resources'])
